  "live_records": {
    "q-103136": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103136",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡4\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $4\\pi $ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -2\\pi  & =2\\pi  \\\\ 2\\pi -2\\pi  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\operatorname{sec}⁡4\\pi .$\n\nThe angle $0$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253609/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
