    "q-85510": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85510",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{7\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe angle $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253609/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2})=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
