    "q-103144": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103144",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{5\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253609/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
