    "q-103159": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103159",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253609/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
